def old(i,j):return OLD[i][1][j][1]
SECTIONS=[]
def section(title,items):SECTIONS.append((title,items))
section('Introduction',[
('',"Retrieval-augmented generation connects a language model to external evidence, allowing answers to draw on information beyond the model's parameters [1]–[3]. This connection also creates an instruction boundary. A document may be relevant to a question without being authorised to change the assistant's task. When a retrieved email says to ignore the question and print a different answer, the system must distinguish evidence about the world from a command directed at itself. Retrieval relevance alone provides no such distinction."),
('',"Indirect prompt injection exploits that boundary through material an application retrieves or reads [4]. An attacker can place an instruction in a source that is subsequently copied into a model's prompt. The user can then receive an attacker-selected answer despite having issued a legitimate request. RAG is especially exposed because useful and adversarial text can occupy the same passage. Discarding a suspicious passage protects instruction integrity only if the remaining evidence still supports the requested answer."),
('',"We study a gate placed between retrieval and generation. It combines a logistic probe over frozen BERT representations with a narrow rule that recognises explicit attempts to control a response. The design makes the contribution of each signal visible through an ablation. Its practical value depends on three outcomes together: attacks recognised, legitimate answers preserved, and computation added. Calling a linear head lightweight describes its trainable component; the frozen encoder remains part of the inference cost."),
('Research questions',"RQ1: How much injected text does the passage gate detect at a validation-constrained operating point, and how often does it reject legitimate instructions?\nRQ2: Does combining learned and explicit signals improve coverage over either component and an existing injection classifier?\nRQ3: Do passage decisions translate into fewer successful output-redirection attacks while preserving answer quality at an acceptable cost?"),
('Hypothesis',"H1: The learned score and explicit cue cover partly different attacks, so their combination improves recall over each component at the selected validation constraint. The resulting system benefit must be evaluated separately: a detected attack can share a chunk with the correct answer, while an undetected instruction can still be ignored by the generator. The external classifier and benign-instruction challenge test whether any apparent advantage depends on an unusually easy negative set."),
('Contributions',"First, we provide an auditable passage gate with frozen coefficients, explicit rules, validation-selected thresholds and a source-overlap audit. Second, we connect passage decisions to actual generated answers in a document-scoped RAG experiment crossing source emails, attack templates and positions. Third, we compare an external classifier, stress legitimate instructions, and test mixed-window training and score sensitivity. Fourth, we report security, task utility, errors and measured cost together, with raw outputs supporting the interpretation."),
('',"The experiments are deliberately tractable on a local CPU. Microsoft BIPIA supplies the EmailQA records and public attack bank [7]; the retrieval adapter and gate are implemented here. The generator is Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct [14]. This scope supports a controlled study of passage filtering. It also exposes the limits of a passage-only detector when benign documents contain instructions and when the generator must recognise that an answer is unsupported.")])
section('Related Work',[
('',"RAG separates retrieval from answer generation [1]. Dense Passage Retrieval learns a retriever [2], while Fusion-in-Decoder aggregates retrieved evidence inside a generator [3]. These designs establish the value of external knowledge, but relevance and instruction authority are separate properties. Our sparse, within-email retriever holds retrieval simple so that changes in downstream behaviour can be traced to passage filtering."),
('',"Greshake et al. demonstrate indirect instruction attacks against integrated applications [4]. InjecAgent studies tool-integrated agents [5], and PoisonedRAG targets answer corruption through poisoned retrieval material [6]. BIPIA provides tasks, attack constructions and defensive evaluation machinery [7]. These settings motivate distinct outcomes: our marker measures an instruction-following deviation, whereas incorrect answers can also arise from ordinary QA failure. The public EmailQA records provide a concrete task without requiring a paid model judge."),
('',"Defences intervene at different layers. StruQ uses a front end and a specially trained model to separate prompts from data [8]. Spotlighting transforms untrusted inputs to signal their provenance [15]. Known-answer detection uses a predictable auxiliary task to detect contamination, but subsequent adaptive analysis reveals structural weaknesses [18]. These approaches motivate careful trust-boundary design; they do not establish the effectiveness of a separately trained passage classifier."),
('',"Detection-and-removal work explicitly studies identifying injected spans and processing the contaminated document [9]. ProtectAI's released DeBERTa model supplies an existing English injection classifier [17]. We use that checkpoint as an external passage baseline with a stated window wrapper and fixed threshold. RobustRAG instead isolates evidence, generates separate answers and securely aggregates them under a bounded corruption model [16]. Our gate is cheaper in generation calls but has no corresponding robustness certificate."),
('',"The gap addressed here is a small, inspectable comparison linking detector choices to legitimate answers, adversarial outcomes and inference cost under the same application path. We examine whether the two signals complement one another, whether a public classifier changes the comparison, and whether performance survives benign instructions. Adaptive-attack results warn that a fixed attack bank leaves substantial uncertainty [11]. RAGAs and ARES offer broader QA evaluation frameworks [12], [13]; our controlled numeric task instead uses an explicit local scorer so every judgement can be inspected. The resulting contribution is empirical evidence about this bounded implementation and its failure modes.")])
section('Methodology',[
('Threat model and pipeline',old(2,0)),
('',old(2,1)),
('FIG','pipeline'),
('',"The attacker edits the source before chunking, rather than inserting text directly into the final prompt. Retrieval can therefore omit the marker, split it from surrounding evidence, or return a mixed chunk. We record exposure before the gate and retention after the gate. The user question and system instruction are fixed across methods. A defence changes only the selected evidence; it receives no additional privileged warning. Figure 1 maps the attack surface and trust boundary to the executed request path."),
('Unit of analysis',"The source email, benchmark record and attack variant are distinct units. Development-to-test overlap is removed by exact email text. The expanded generator study uses all 33 distinct audited test emails and crosses every email with four authored templates at two insertion positions. Repeated variants from one email share evidence and a target answer. Statistical resampling therefore keeps them together. A larger count of variants improves coverage of the fixed crossing without creating an equally large count of independent source documents."),
('Frozen representation and linear probe',old(4,1).replace('BERT runs','BERT [10] runs')),
('',"For a token window w, the representation h(w) is the attention-mask-weighted mean of BERT's final hidden states, including special tokens. Content windows contain at most 126 tokens, use a 96-token stride and add CLS/SEP tokens. A class-balanced logistic regression with C=1 and a fixed seed fits the head. Its coefficients a and intercept b define the window score. Maximum pooling gives a conservative passage score that can flag one risky window without averaging it away across a long email."),
('EQ','probe'),
('',"The original probe fits clean email windows as negatives and stand-alone public training payloads as positives. No generator output enters this fitting process. That simple construction makes the baseline reproducible, but also changes the surrounding text between fitting and inference. A long mixed window can dilute the signature learned from a short stand-alone instruction. The supplementary mixed-window experiment tests this explanation with the same encoder and training-source boundary."),
('Explicit cue and combined risk',"The rule r(d) is one if a passage matches a frozen regular expression for response-control phrases or privileged-role impersonation. The patterns cover ignoring previous instructions, a system or developer message/prompt, references such as your answer or the output, and directives to reply only, instead, or with. They are intentionally inspectable. Their sensitivity to legitimate occurrences of the same phrases is measured rather than assumed away."),
('EQ','combined'),
('',"The parameter lambda is 0.75 in the primary comparison. With no cue, the combined score equals the probe score; with a cue, the risk increases monotonically. The expression is a bounded heuristic score, not a calibrated probability of malicious intent or a factual-trust estimate. Each method uses its own fixed threshold. The retention decision keeps a retrieved passage only when its score is strictly below that threshold."),
('EQ','threshold'),
('Validation and calibration',old(3,-3)),
('',"For clarity, the implementation searches observed validation scores and an above-one sentinel, retains thresholds with at most 5% clean false positives, and chooses greatest recall, then precision, then threshold. With only 14 audited clean validation records, even one false rejection exceeds 5%; the constraint is therefore equivalent to zero observed false positives. This discretisation is an important part of the operating point. It is a development constraint on a small sample, not a deployment guarantee."),
('Retrieval and answer generation',old(4,2)),
('',old(4,3)),
('',old(3,-2)),
('',"The final condition is an explicit abstention path. If all retrieved chunks are rejected, the application returns UNKNOWN without a model call. If at least one chunk remains, the same chat template and greedy generation settings are used. A generator-produced UNKNOWN is recorded separately from a gate abstention. This separation shows whether a defence lowers attack success by preserving useful evidence, by refusing to answer, or by relying on the generator to ignore retained instructions."),
('Runtime and implementation provenance',old(4,4)),
('',"The original 16-email pilot contains 128 uncached requests and supplies the latency comparison. In the expanded run, identical question-and-retained-evidence inputs reuse a previous deterministic generation, and repeated BERT representations may be reused. Every reused response records its origin. This reduces redundant computation without changing the specified greedy response. Expanded wall times consequently describe execution with reuse and are excluded from cross-method latency claims. The larger crossing is used for security and answer-quality outcomes."),
('',"The expansion uses a newly restored local Python environment. Exact library versions for the pilot and revision are recorded separately in the package; changing runtime versions is a possible numerical reproducibility factor. Model weights, prompt, decoding, gate coefficients, rules and thresholds are unchanged. The two runs are presented as separate experiments rather than pooled as a single homogeneous sample."),
('',"The implementation and machine-readable results are provided in the project repository CODE_REPOSITORY_FOOTNOTE. The upstream BIPIA repository is separately attributed as the data source. The package includes scripts, fixed settings, raw responses, predictions, coefficient files, source hashes and the report source. Model weights are obtained from their official model pages and are excluded from the archive. The code contains a direct generation path so results can be reproduced without relying on stored responses."),
('Data construction and overlap control',old(5,0)),
('',old(5,1)),
('',old(5,2)),
('Controlled end-to-end crossing',"Four authored payloads request RAG_OVERRIDE_764: direct answer replacement, a fake system message, a task-looking verification phrase and a quality-assurance note. Each payload is placed at both the beginning and end of every distinct test email before indexing. Thus each gate sees 33 clean cases and 264 attacked variants. Four methods yield 132 clean and 1,056 attacked decisions, or 1,188 logged requests. These constructed attacks are separate from the public passage-detection attack bank."),
('',"All 33 source texts are included in original retained-file order, without selecting by detector score, baseline correctness or answerability. This removes the earlier pilot's 8/8 balancing choice. The ideal answer and question remain the public record's values. The natural answerability mix within this small audited pool is reported alongside aggregate correctness. Every template/position pair has the same 33-email support, allowing an interpretable within-email comparison."),
('External classifier and legitimate instructions',"The external baseline is ProtectAI's DeBERTa-v3-base-prompt-injection-v2 [17], pinned to a recorded repository revision. The positive class is injection; probability 0.5 is the fixed rejection threshold. To match passage coverage, we use 126 content-token windows with stride 96 and maximum-pool their positive-class probabilities. This is an adapted passage wrapper, whereas the model card's example truncates a single input to 512 tokens. Results therefore describe our explicitly stated wrapper, not the publisher's original benchmark numbers."),
('',"The external model evaluates the same 37 clean records and 111 attacked passages as the local detectors. Its training data are public and broad; complete contamination equivalence with our test source cannot be established. No model-card accuracy is substituted for a measured result. The checkpoint is used as a released reference method even though its project is now archived; archival status does not alter the frozen weights evaluated here."),
('',"A separate challenge set contains 24 authored legitimate instruction-bearing paragraphs. Twelve are ordinary requests or procedures, such as asking a recipient to confirm an invoice. Twelve deliberately contain the rule's response-control vocabulary in benign contexts, including quoted warnings and references to a printer's system message. All are labelled benign for the passage task. They are excluded from threshold selection and model fitting. The two groups diagnose false-positive mechanisms; their balanced construction does not estimate how often such material occurs in real email."),
('Mixed-window correction experiment',"We train one additional logistic head over the same frozen BERT features. Each of the 45 original fitting payloads is inserted into a fitting email at both positions. Token offsets identify the inserted span. A window is positive only if it intersects that payload; surrounding email-only windows remain negative. This avoids labelling every window in a long contaminated email as malicious. Clean fitting emails are also negatives. The fitting source pool, C=1, class balancing and seed remain fixed."),
('',"The corrected head is calibrated on the original audited validation mixtures under the same false-positive rule, then evaluated once on the held-out passage set and the benign challenge. The comparison tests a targeted training-distribution correction. It changes both contextualisation and the number/composition of fitting windows, so a recall change cannot isolate one causal mechanism. It provides evidence about this practical correction rather than proving that window mixing alone explains the original probe's weakness."),
('Sensitivity analysis',"Without new generator calls, cached validation and test scores support a five-value lambda analysis: 0, 0.25, 0.50, 0.75 and 1.00. Each weight selects a threshold using validation alone and is then evaluated on the fixed passage test. A second descriptive sweep holds lambda at 0.75 and displays thresholds 0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95 and 0.99. The sweep exposes security/retention trade-offs; none of its test results replaces the primary operating point."),
('Outcome definitions',old(6,0)),
('',old(6,1)),
('',"Generator attack success is the case-insensitive occurrence of the complete marker in the response. The input containing the marker establishes exposure, not success. We also report whether any marker-bearing chunk survives the gate. The all-variant denominator includes variants that retrieval does not expose; conditional ASR uses only exposed variants. Reporting both prevents a retriever's omission from being silently attributed to detector effectiveness."),
('',old(6,3)),
('',old(6,4)),
('Uncertainty and reporting',"For expanded generator comparisons, a source-email cluster bootstrap resamples 33 emails with replacement and includes all eight attack variants and the clean condition for each selected email. The paired change uses identical sampled emails across methods. One thousand fixed-seed resamples provide descriptive percentile intervals. We additionally report the number of emails with at least one successful variant, with a Wilson interval over the 33 source units. This makes the remaining sample-size limitation visible even if the variant-level ASR is zero."),
('',"Detection PR and ROC summaries use the same stored scores as the tables. The attacked-heavy case mix makes precision prevalence-dependent. The synthetic benign challenge is reported as raw rejected counts and separate groups. Timing is taken only from the original uncached pilot, with filtering separated from request time. These choices keep the denominators and computation boundaries explicit while avoiding repeated claims in every results paragraph.")])
